var e=`seven-day-upanishadic-reset`,t=`A 7-Day Upanishadic Reset (Educational, Not Therapy)`,n=`October 2026`,r=`9 min read`,i=[`Practice`,`Daily Reset`,`Contemplation`,`Education`],a=[15,0],o=[`taittiriya-2-9-1`,`katha-1-3-14`,`chandogya-6-8-7`],s=`A structured, 7-day contemplative exploration applying Upanishadic principles to daily attention, somatic grounding, cognitive discernment, and silent reflection. Educational only.`,c=`Classical Vedantic pedagogical structure: Śravaṇa (attentive listening), Manana (rational reflection), and Nididhyāsana (experiential integration), adapted for contemporary personal study.`,l=`Educational contemplative guide only. Not clinical mental health therapy or treatment for psychiatric disorders. If experiencing acute distress, seek qualified medical support.`,u=[{heading:`Structure of the Reset`,paragraphs:[`This 7-day reset offers an invitation to pause the relentless acceleration of contemporary digital life and explore the contemplative technologies of the Upanishads. Each day pairs a verified Sanskrit mantra, a reflective inquiry, and a gentle micro-practice.`,`Remember: do not rush. The Upanishads are meant to be tasted slowly, like water sipped from a mountain spring.`]},{heading:`The Daily Pathway`,paragraphs:[`Day 1: Saha Nāvavatu (The Container of Safety)
• Verse: Taittirīya Śānti Pāṭha
• Focus: Before solving any crisis, establish shared ontological safety with those around you. Practice non-adversarial dialogue.

Day 2: Abhaya (Fear Needs a Second)
• Verse: Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.4.2
• Focus: Notice where you perceive an "other" today. Can you recognize the shared consciousness behind the person who irritates you?

Day 3: The Charioteer Awakes (Executive Mastery)
• Verse: Kaṭha 1.3.3
• Focus: Observe the sensory horses of your smartphone and appetites. Hand the reins back to your quiet intellect (buddhi).

Day 4: Titikṣā (Patience Without Resentment)
• Verse: Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.23
• Focus: When delayed in traffic or encountering an inconvenience, practice dropping the inner complaint. Endure with majestic calm.

Day 5: Tat Tvam Asi (Identity That Doesn't Break)
• Verse: Chāndogya 6.8.7
• Focus: Notice the difference between your temporary social roles (employee, parent, consumer) and the silent awareness that witnesses them.

Day 6: The Witness Bird (Watching Without Tasting)
• Verse: Muṇḍaka 3.1.1
• Focus: Spend 10 minutes observing your stream of thoughts like clouds passing through an indifferent sky.

Day 7: Asato Mā Sad Gamaya (Ascent to the Light)
• Verse: Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.3.28
• Focus: Re-enter your life with the quiet knowing: "The storm moves the tree. It does not move the ground."`]},{heading:`Final Compassionate Counsel`,paragraphs:[`Resilience is not a trophy you win; it is an unshakeable ground you remember. Whenever the world shakes you, return to your breath, return to the witness, and remember that you are that which was never broken.`]}],d=[`Gambhirananda, Swami. Eight Upanishads with the Commentary of Shankaracharya. Advaita Ashrama, 1989.`,`Saraswati, Swami Dayananda. Introduction to Vedanta: Understanding the Fundamental Problem. Vision Books, 2005.`,`Rao, K. Ramakrishna. Consciousness Studies in the Indian Tradition. Indian Council of Philosophical Research, 2002.`],f={slug:e,title:t,date:n,readingTime:r,tags:i,relatedChapters:a,relatedVerses:o,summary:s,viewOrigin:c,limits:l,sections:u,sources:d};export{n as date,f as default,l as limits,r as readingTime,a as relatedChapters,o as relatedVerses,u as sections,e as slug,d as sources,s as summary,i as tags,t as title,c as viewOrigin};